-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- DO NOT RUN. NEUTRALISED 2026-09-03. Renamed off `.sql` so no migration runner,
-- glob, or `psql -f migrations/*.sql` can pick it up. Kept, not deleted, because
-- the reasoning below is the reusable part and a deleted file gets reinvented.
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE DOES, in its own words: it takes three attestation UIDs copied
-- from the Base Sepolia EAS explorer and writes them onto ten of our own rows, so
-- that a report could link to easscan and look verified. Its comments say so
-- plainly -- "real example UIDs from Base Sepolia EAS explorer for verifiability
-- in report", "makes the historical anchored proofs 'honest' for presentProof()
-- demo".
--
-- Those attestations are real and they are NOT OURS. They attest nothing about
-- any agent in this system. A row carrying one asserts an on-chain fact it did
-- not earn, and it asserts it in the most convincing possible form: a UID that
-- resolves in a public explorer. That is worse than an obvious stub. A stub
-- (`eas-stub-<ts>-<id>`) announces itself; a valid 66-character UID that loads
-- in easscan reads as proof to every human and every script that follows it.
--
-- It also writes by `LIMIT 4` / `LIMIT 3` / `LIMIT 3` against an unordered
-- predicate, so WHICH rows got which borrowed UID was whatever the planner
-- returned -- unrepeatable, and unattributable afterwards.
--
-- IT WAS NEVER APPLIED TO PRODUCTION [MEASURED 2026-09-03]: zero rows in
-- `repid_zkp_proofs` carry any of the three UIDs below. So there is nothing to
-- clean up. The hazard was that the file sat in `migrations/` looking like an
-- ordinary pending backfill that someone would eventually, reasonably, run.
--
-- WHAT WAS FOUND INSTEAD, while checking: five rows DO carry a uid while failing
-- the anchoring eligibility rule. Those five are a different and much smaller
-- problem -- the UIDs are genuinely ours (this system's attester and schema, on
-- chain, timestamps matching the rows), minted for proofs marked `is_real=false`
-- in one 63-second window on 2026-05-30 and never repeated since. They are now
-- reported as `ANCHORED_INELIGIBLE` rather than `ANCHORED`
-- (`src/services/anchor-status.ts`). Do not conflate the two: this file would
-- have borrowed someone else's evidence; those five over-claimed with our own.
--
-- If a demo needs anchored proofs, MINT THEM. The path exists and costs cents:
-- `easAnchorWorker` batches to a Merkle root, and one attestation covering 100
-- proofs measured at roughly $0.0066 in gas. There is no argument for borrowing.
--
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- ORIGINAL CONTENT BELOW, UNMODIFIED AND INERT.
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

-- S-ONCHAIN Phase 2: One-time backfill to populate eas_attestation_uid for >=10 existing qualifying proofs (those with merkle_root from drain).
-- This makes the historical 4,573 anchored proofs "honest" for presentProof() demo.
-- Real future proofs will get uid at insert time via the wired easService in proof-drain-service.ts .
-- Run via apply_migration or Supabase SQL editor (after schema verify).
-- UIDs are real from Base Sepolia EAS recent attestations (for demo; replace with ones from actual attest run with funded key if needed).

